        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "198a85a2-2933-5ec8-84fe-f614926a202e",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Events: Mudan incident of 1871\n- People: Ryūkyūan people, Ryūkyūan sailors\n- Places: Qing-era Taiwan, central Taiwan, Ryukyu Islands, Kyushu, Taiwan\n\nFacts:\n- The Mudan incident of 1871 was the massacre of 54 Ryūkyūan sailors in Qing-era Taiwan.\n- The 54 Ryūkyūan sailors reached central Taiwan after their ship was shipwrecked.\n- The Ryūkyūan people are the indigenous peoples of the Ryukyu Islands.\n- The Ryukyu Islands lie between Kyushu and Taiwan.\n- The Ryūkyūan people are also called Lewchewan, Uchinaanchu, Okinawa jin, and Ryūkyū minzoku.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "4f9c58ec-22e6-5ebd-b6be-4b6948c38922",
        "text": "Page: Mudan incident (1871). Sentence: The Mudan incident of 1871 was the massacre of 54 Ryūkyūan sailors in Qing-era Taiwan who wandered into the central part of Taiwan after their ship was shipwrecked.\nPage: Ryukyuan people. Sentence: The Ryukyuan people (琉球民族 , Ryūkyū minzoku , Okinawan: \"Ruuchuu minzuku\") (also Lewchewan or Uchinaanchu (沖縄人 , Japanese: \"Okinawa jin\") ) are the indigenous peoples of the Ryukyu Islands between the islands of Kyushu and Taiwan.",
